Description: One Num Lock for the seat, set and remembered by the server
 Mir keeps lock state per keyboard, starts every keyboard with Num Lock
 off and gives the shell no way to change it, so Umoja could not restore
 GNOME's remembered Num Lock (org.gnome.desktop.peripherals.keyboard
 remember-numlock-state / numlock-state). If the server executable exports
 a C function named mir_num_lock_initial_hook, the key mapper asks it once
 whether Num Lock starts on, and from then on keeps one Num Lock for the
 whole seat like mutter: every keyboard, including ones plugged in later,
 follows it, toggling it on one keyboard toggles it (and the LED) on all
 of them, and mir_num_lock_changed_hook, if exported, hears each change so
 the server can save it. Without the hooks nothing changes; no ABI change
 (private members of an internal class only).
Author: Umoja developers
Forwarded: no

